<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Guided Lecture Notes worksheet: "Classifying Differential Equations"
	Book sections (Exploring Differential Equations, geoff-cox/debookrs):
		c1-classification/sec-order.ptx
		c1-classification/sec-linear-terms.ptx
		c1-classification/sec-linearity.ptx
	Old deck: source/notes/latex/ 01 intro-topics folder (order, linearity)

	Worksheet conventions (see CLAUDE.md section 4 and ws-what-is-a-de.ptx):
	  * Student/instructor split via component="stu" / component="key",
	    selected by <source><version include="notes stu|key"/> in the
	    publication files. Both versions mirror each other in structure.
	  * Reading-check blanks: paired <p component="stu"> (with <fillin/>)
	    and <p component="key"> (answers as underlined bold math).
	  * Worked problems: ONE <exercise> whose <statement> holds the prompt,
	    then <p component="stu" workspace="Xin"/> (generous write-in space),
	    then <p component="key"> with the answer.  No <solution>, and no
	    duplicated stu/key exercise pair.
	  * <page> delimits vertical space for letter-size printing, NOT topics.
	    Headings are <p><term>...</term></p>, never page titles.
	  * Display math uses <md>; <me>/<men> are deprecated.
  -->
<worksheet xml:id="ws-classification">
	<title>Classifying Differential Equations</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li><p><term>Determine</term> the order of a differential equation.</p></li>
			<li><p><term>Classify</term> individual terms as linear or nonlinear.</p></li>
			<li><p><term>Decide</term> whether a whole differential equation is linear or nonlinear.</p></li>
			<li><p><term>Apply</term> these labels to differential equations that model real systems.</p></li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<page>
		<p><term>Classifying a differential equation: order and linearity.</term></p>

		<p>
			Once we can name the parts of an equation, two labels let us predict which
			solution methods will work: its <term>order</term> and whether it is
			<term>linear</term>.
		</p>

		<p>
			<term>Reading check.</term> Fill in each blank from your reading.
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p component="stu">
						The <term>order</term> of a differential equation is the
						<fillin characters="12"/> derivative of the dependent variable that
						appears in it.
					</p>
					<p component="key">
						The <term>order</term> of a differential equation is the
						<m>\underline{\textbf{highest}}</m> derivative of the dependent
						variable that appears in it.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p component="stu">
						A single term is <term>linear</term> if it has one of the forms
						<m>a(t)</m>, <m>a(t)\,y</m>, or <m>a(t)\,y^{(n)}</m>, where the
						coefficient <m>a(t)</m> depends on the <fillin characters="13"/>
						variable only. The coefficient, <m>a(t)</m>, (does | does not)
						affect linearity.
					</p>
					<p component="key">
						A single term is <term>linear</term> if it has one of the forms
						<m>a(t)</m>, <m>a(t)\,y</m>, or <m>a(t)\,y^{(n)}</m>, where the
						coefficient <m>a(t)</m> depends on the
						<m>\underline{\textbf{independent}}</m> variable only. The coefficient,
						<m>a(t)</m>, <m>\underline{\textbf{does not}}</m> affect linearity.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p component="stu">
						A whole differential equation is linear when <fillin characters="8"/>
						term is linear; it is nonlinear as soon as <fillin characters="8"/>
						term is nonlinear.
					</p>
					<p component="key">
						A whole differential equation is linear when
						<m>\underline{\textbf{every}}</m> term is linear; it is nonlinear as
						soon as <m>\underline{\textbf{one}}</m> term is nonlinear.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

		<exercise xml:id="ws-cl-ex-tf-linearity">
			<title>True or False</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A differential equation is linear if the majority of its terms are linear.
				</p>
				<p component="key">
					False. Linearity is all-or-nothing: one nonlinear term is enough to
					make the entire equation nonlinear.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ws-cl-ex-linear-terms-key">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Give the order and label each term of the equation as linear or nonlinear. If nonlinear, explain why.
					<md>
						y'\,\frac{d^3 y}{dt^3} + 5t^4\,\frac{dy}{dt}
						+ \cos(y) + (y'')^2 = \sqrt{t}
					</md>
				</p>
				<p component="stu"><term>Order:</term></p>
        <p component="key"><term>Order:</term> <m>\underline{\text{Third-order since highest derivative is}\ d^3y/dt^3}</m>.</p>
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <col halign="center" width="14%"/>
          <col halign="center" width="14%"/>
          <col halign="left" width="72%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><p>Term</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Linearity</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Justification</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>y'\dfrac{d^3y}{dt^3}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><em>nonlinear</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Two derivatives of the dependent variable are multiplied together.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>5t^4\dfrac{dy}{dt}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key"><em>linear</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key">Has the form <m>a(t)\,y'</m>; the coefficient <m>5t^4</m> is irrelevant.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\cos(y)\vphantom{\dfrac{d}{d}}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key"><em>nonlinear</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key">The dependent variable sits inside a nonlinear function.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>(y'')^2\vphantom{\dfrac{d}{d}}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key"><em>nonlinear</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key">A derivative is raised to a power other than one.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\sqrt{t}\vphantom{\dfrac{d}{d}}</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key"><em>linear</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="stu"/><p component="key">No dependent variable appears (has the form: <m>a(t)y^{0}</m>).</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
			</statement>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ws-cl-ex-linearity">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Determine the dependent variable, order, and linearity for each differential equation.
				</p>
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <col halign="center" width="40%"/>
          <col halign="center" width="12%"/>
          <col halign="center" width="12%"/>
          <col halign="center" width="12%"/>
          <col halign="left" width="24%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><p>Equation</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Dep Var</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Order</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Linear?</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Nonlinear terms</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\displaystyle w^{(4)} + \dfrac{w'}{t^3} + 9w = \tan t</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>w</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key">4th</p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><em>Yes</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><mdash /></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\displaystyle Q^{(5)} + \dfrac{k\,Q''}{Q'} = (k + 2)^3</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>Q</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key">5th</p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><em>No</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>\dfrac{k\,Q''}{Q'}</m></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\displaystyle \dfrac{d^3 B}{ds^3} + s^2\dfrac{dB}{ds} + B = 42</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>B</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key">3rd</p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><em>Yes</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><mdash /></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><m>\displaystyle v^8 + \cos\!\big(v^{(6)}\big) = 0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>v</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key">6th</p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><em>No</em></p></cell>
            <cell><p component="key"><m>v^8</m>, <m>\cos\!\big(v^{(6)}\big)</m></p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
			</statement>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="ws-cl-ex-modeling">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>Classifying models.</term> For each equation below, give its 
					<em>order</em> and say whether it is <em>linear</em> or 
					<em>nonlinear</em>.
					<ol>
						<li>
							<p>Skydiver with air resistance: <m>\quad m\dfrac{dv}{dt} = mg - cv^2</m></p>
							<p component="stu" workspace="1.0in"/>
							<p component="key"><term>First-order, nonlinear.</term></p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>IV drug infusion: <m>\quad \dfrac{dA}{dt} = r - kA</m></p>
							<p component="stu" workspace="1.0in"/>
							<p component="key"><term>First-order, linear.</term></p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>RLC circuit charge: <m>\quad L\dfrac{d^2 q}{dt^2} + R\dfrac{dq}{dt} + \dfrac{1}{C}q = E(t)</m></p>
							<p component="stu" workspace="1.0in"/>
							<p component="key"><term>Second-order, linear.</term></p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>Self-sustaining oscillator (van der Pol): <m>\quad \dfrac{d^2 x}{dt^2} - \mu\left(1 - x^2\right)\dfrac{dx}{dt} + x = 0</m></p>
							<p component="stu" workspace="1.0in"/>
							<p component="key"><term>Second-order, nonlinear.</term></p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</exercise>

    <p>
      <term>Looking ahead</term>. Order and linearity are the two labels we will
      read off <em>first</em>
      for every new equation, because together they decide which technique to
      reach for. Next we turn to what it even means to <em>solve</em> a
      differential equation and how an initial condition pins down the one
      solution we want out of the whole family.
    </p>
	</page>
</worksheet>